    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d358">

                <pb facs="1051" n="1035" xml:id="pg1035"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source"> S/S–NSC Files, Lot 63 D 351, NSC 104–Supplemental
                    Reports</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Executive Secretary of the National
                        Security Council</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lay</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the National Security Council</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d358-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-02-12">February 12,
                        1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: U.S. Policies and Programs in the Economic Field
                    Which May Affect the War Potential of the Soviet Bloc</p>
                <p>Reference: NSC 104<anchor n="2" xml:id="d358-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>The enclosed copies of an analysis of the “Vulnerability of the Soviet Bloc to
                    Existing and Tightened Western Economic Controls”, prepared in the Department of
                    State (Appendix A), and of an analysis of the “Trade of the Free World With the
                    Soviet Bloc”, prepared by the Economic Cooperation Administration (Appendix B),
                    are transmitted herewith for the information of the National Security Council in
                    connection with its consideration of NSC 104 on the subject.</p>
                <p>Both the enclosures are referred to in the second paragraph of the letter from
                    the Secretary of State to the President contained in NSC 104.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d358-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">James</hi> S. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lay,
                            Jr</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>




                <xi:include href="d358-a1.xml" xpointer="d358-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d358-a2.xml" xpointer="d358-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d358-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d358-f1-content">
                <p>Copies were also sent to the Secretary of the Treasury, Secretary of the
                    Interior, Secretary of Agriculture, Secretary of Commerce, Director of Defense
                    Mobilization, Administrator for Economic Cooperation, and the Director of the
                    Bureau of the Budget.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d358-f2" n="2" xml:id="d358-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg1023">p. 1023</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d358-f3" n="3" xml:id="d358-f3-content">
                <p>Enclosure 2 to NSC 104, February 12, <ref target="#pg1025">p. 1025</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
